Bluetooth: Controller: An unframed SDU lost on a CIS is reported to the host

The CIS link layer enqueues only the PDUs it received, so a payload that was never received leaves
nothing behind but a jump in the next PDU's payload number. The unframed ISO-AL ignored that jump
and numbered only the SDUs it emitted, so the host never learned that an SDU was missing.
PipeWire numbers received audio by the ISO sequence number and conceals a gap with LC3 PLC.
Without the gap it played the next SDU in the lost one's place, its ISO clock slipped by an
interval, and its rate control kept pulling the pitch down and back up. The BIS receiver does not
have this problem because its link layer enqueues an error PDU for each payload it missed.

The ISO-AL now releases each SDU in such a jump as lost data (Packet_Status_Flag 0b10, no data),
one SDU interval after the previous one, so every SDU interval reaches the host with its own
sequence number. After a jump of more than 255 SDUs it releases 255 and only advances the
sequence number for the rest.

Steps to reproduce:
1. Pair a Pi 5 with a BE200 to the VIM3L with the nRF54L15 DK as its controller, over LE.
2. Play music from the Pi 5 to the VIM3L over BAP unicast and listen to it in the VIM3L's console.
3. The pitch wobbles all the time. The DK's HCI trace shows ISO data packets 15 or 22.5 ms apart
   in its own timestamps while the sequence numbers count up by one.

Applied to the workspace's Zephyr tree (ZEPHYR_VERSION) by `make nrf-hci`.

diff --git a/subsys/bluetooth/controller/ll_sw/isoal.c b/subsys/bluetooth/controller/ll_sw/isoal.c
index 9e7e1215857..c1c77d78de5 100644
--- a/subsys/bluetooth/controller/ll_sw/isoal.c
+++ b/subsys/bluetooth/controller/ll_sw/isoal.c
@@ -714,6 +714,67 @@ static isoal_status_t isoal_rx_append_to_sdu(struct isoal_sink *sink,
 }
 
 
+/* Release the SDUs whose PDUs never reached the ISO-AL, unframed
+ *
+ * A CIS link layer only enqueues the PDUs it received: a payload that was
+ * flushed (not received, CRC errors, no free Rx node) leaves no trace but a
+ * jump in the payload number of the next PDU. Each SDU in that jump is released
+ * as lost data, one SDU interval after the previous one, so the host sees every
+ * SDU interval with its own sequence number and can conceal the gap (a host
+ * that numbers the audio by the sequence number otherwise plays the next SDU in
+ * the lost one's place and runs short).
+ *
+ * BT Core V5.3 : Vol 4 HCI I/F : Part E HCI Func. Spec.:
+ * 5.4.5 HCI ISO Data packets: Packet_Status_Flag 0b10, no data received.
+ */
+static isoal_status_t isoal_rx_unframed_release_lost_sdus(struct isoal_sink *sink,
+							  const struct isoal_pdu_rx *pdu_meta)
+{
+	struct isoal_sink_session *session;
+	struct isoal_sdu_production *sp;
+	struct isoal_sdu_produced *sdu;
+	uint64_t sdu_prev, sdu_next, lost_sdus;
+	isoal_status_t err;
+
+	sp = &sink->sdu_production;
+	session = &sink->session;
+	sdu = &sp->sdu;
+	err = ISOAL_STATUS_OK;
+
+	if (!sp->initialized) {
+		return err;
+	}
+
+	sdu_prev = sp->prev_pdu_id / session->pdus_per_sdu;
+	sdu_next = pdu_meta->meta->payload_number / session->pdus_per_sdu;
+	if (sdu_next <= sdu_prev + 1U) {
+		return err;
+	}
+	lost_sdus = sdu_next - sdu_prev - 1U;
+
+	/* After a long gap, release a few and only count the rest: the host
+	 * learns of them from the sequence number, without a burst of empty
+	 * packets holding up the HCI.
+	 */
+	if (lost_sdus > UINT8_MAX) {
+		session->sn += lost_sdus - UINT8_MAX;
+		lost_sdus = UINT8_MAX;
+	}
+
+	ISOAL_LOG_DBGV("[%p] Releasing %u lost SDUs", sink, (uint32_t)lost_sdus);
+
+	while (lost_sdus > 0U && !err) {
+		sdu->timestamp = isoal_get_wrapped_time_us(sdu->timestamp, session->sdu_interval);
+		sp->sdu_state = BT_ISO_START;
+		sp->sdu_status = ISOAL_SDU_STATUS_LOST_DATA;
+
+		err = isoal_rx_append_to_sdu(sink, pdu_meta, 0, 0, true, false);
+		lost_sdus--;
+	}
+
+	return err;
+}
+
 /**
  * @brief Consume an unframed PDU: Copy contents into SDU(s) and emit to a sink
  * @details Destination sink may have an already partially built SDU
@@ -796,6 +857,8 @@ static isoal_status_t isoal_rx_unframed_consume(struct isoal_sink *sink,
 		uint16_t sdu_offset;
 		int32_t latency;
 
+		err |= isoal_rx_unframed_release_lost_sdus(sink, pdu_meta);
+
 		sp->sdu_status = ISOAL_SDU_STATUS_VALID;
 		sp->sdu_state = BT_ISO_START;
 		sp->pdu_cnt = 1;
